public class Types {
    static final double TAX_RATE = 0.2;           // a constant for the whole class

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int items = 3;
        long bytesOnDisk = 5_000_000_000L;        // too big for an int
        double price = 19.99;
        boolean inStock = true;
        char grade = 'A';
        var total = items * price * (1 + TAX_RATE); // var: the compiler picks double

        System.out.println(total);                // 71.964
        System.out.println(2_147_483_647 + 1);    // -2147483648 (the int wrapped around!)
        System.out.println(bytesOnDisk + " " + inStock + " " + grade);
    }
}

public class Ops {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(7 / 2);           // 3   (integer division)
        System.out.println(7 / 2.0);         // 3.5
        System.out.println(7 % 3);           // 1
        System.out.println((int) 3.99);      // 3   (cast cuts off the fraction)
        System.out.println(Math.round(3.5)); // 4

        int score = 10;
        score += 5;                          // 15
        int total = 30, count = 0;
        boolean high = count != 0 && total / count > 5; // right side skipped: no division by zero
        int bonus = score >= 15 ? 100 : 0;
        System.out.println(score + " " + high + " " + bonus); // 15 false 100
    }
}

public class Strings {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "  Ada Lovelace ";
        String clean = name.strip();                  // "Ada Lovelace"
        name.toUpperCase();                           // result thrown away: name unchanged
        System.out.println(clean.toUpperCase());      // ADA LOVELACE

        String typed = new String("Ada Lovelace");    // same letters, stored separately
        System.out.println(clean == typed);           // false (different addresses)
        System.out.println(clean.equals(typed));      // true  (same letters)

        System.out.println(clean.substring(0, 3));    // Ada
        System.out.println(clean.length() + " characters"); // 12 characters

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append("a").append(',').append("b");
        System.out.println(sb.toString());            // a,b

        String note = """
            Hello, %s
            """.formatted(clean);
        System.out.print(note);                       // Hello, Ada Lovelace
    }
}

concepts.body:
Programs often need to do different things in different situations. A **conditional** runs some code only when a condition is true. The basic form is the `if` statement:

```java
if (temperature > 30) {
    System.out.println("Hot!");
} else if (temperature > 15) {
    System.out.println("Mild");
} else {
    System.out.println("Cold");
}
```

The condition in brackets must be a `boolean`. Each alternative is a **branch**. Java checks the branches from top to bottom and runs the first one whose condition is true; `else` catches everything left over. Always use braces `{ }`, even around a single line. It prevents bugs when someone later adds a second statement.

When you compare one value against many fixed options, use `switch`. Modern Java (14+) has the **arrow form**, which runs exactly one matching case and can produce a value. That makes it a **switch expression**:

```java
String kind = switch (command) {
    case "start", "run" -> "go";
    case "stop" -> "halt";
    default -> "unknown";
};
```

- Several options can share one case, separated by commas.
- A switch expression must cover every possible value, so you usually end with `default`, which catches anything not listed.
- If a case needs several statements, wrap them in braces and hand back the result with `yield`: `case "stop" -> { System.out.println("bye"); yield "halt"; }`.

You can switch on `int`, `char` and `String` values, and on other kinds of value you'll meet later.

The old form uses colons, `case 1:`. After a matching case it keeps running into the next one ("falls through") unless you write `break`. Forgetting `break` is a classic bug, so prefer arrows in new code.

For a simple either-or that produces a value, the ternary `cond ? a : b` from the operators lesson is often clearest. For complicated conditions, store the answer in a well-named boolean variable first: `boolean canVote = age >= 18 && registered;`.

