public class Shop {
    static class Product {              // a class inside Shop; static lets main create it directly
        String name;
        double price;
        int stock;

        boolean sell(int qty) {
            if (qty > stock) return false;
            stock -= qty;
            return true;
        }

        @Override                       // asks the compiler to check we really replace toString
        public String toString() { return name + " ($" + price + ", " + stock + " left)"; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product p = new Product();
        p.name = "Mug"; p.price = 8.5; p.stock = 3;
        Product same = p;               // second reference to the same object
        same.sell(2);
        System.out.println(p);          // Mug ($8.5, 1 left)
        System.out.println(p.sell(5));  // false
    }
}

concepts.body:
A **constructor** is special code that runs when an object is created with `new`, to give its fields their starting values. It looks like a method with the class's name and no return type:

```java
class Account {
    private final String owner;
    private long balanceCents;
    Account(String owner, long openingCents) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balanceCents = openingCents;
    }
}
```

If you write no constructor, the compiler adds a no-argument **default constructor**. As soon as you write any constructor, that default disappears — a common surprise when a **framework** (ready-made code that calls your classes for you) later needs one without arguments.

You can **overload** constructors and have one hand the work to another with `this(...)`, which must be the first statement. Keep the real logic in the most complete constructor and let the others supply defaults:

```java
Account(String owner) { this(owner, 0); }
```

Check (**validate**) arguments in the constructor and fail fast, so an invalid object can never exist. `Objects.requireNonNull(owner, "owner")` and an `IllegalArgumentException` for bad values are the standard tools.

`final` fields must be assigned exactly once, in a constructor or initializer. Making fields final wherever possible gives you objects that are easier to reason about and safe to share between **threads**: parts of one program that run at the same time (covered in a later chapter).

When a class has many optional parameters, long constructor overload chains become unreadable. Options are a static factory method with a descriptive name (`Account.empty(owner)`), a builder (a helper object you configure step by step and then ask for the finished object), or, for plain data, a record (a one-line class for plain data, see the Records lesson), which writes the constructor for you.

Avoid calling, from a constructor, a method that a **subclass** (a class built on top of this one; see Inheritance) could override: the subclass's version would run before the subclass's own fields are set.

