import java.util.Objects;

public class Accounts {
    static class Account {
        private final String owner;
        private long balanceCents;

        Account(String owner, long openingCents) {
            this.owner = Objects.requireNonNull(owner, "owner");
            if (openingCents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative opening balance");
            this.balanceCents = openingCents;
        }
        Account(String owner) { this(owner, 0); }   // delegate with a default

        static Account withBonus(String owner) { return new Account(owner, 10_00); }

        @Override public String toString() { return owner + ": " + balanceCents / 100.0; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Account("Ana"));              // Ana: 0.0
        System.out.println(Account.withBonus("Bo"));         // Bo: 10.0
        new Account("Cy", -5);                               // IllegalArgumentException
    }
}

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Carts {
    static class Cart {
        private final List<String> items = new ArrayList<>();
        private final int maxItems;

        Cart(int maxItems) { this.maxItems = maxItems; }

        public boolean add(String item) {            // behaviour, with a rule
            if (items.size() >= maxItems) return false;
            return items.add(item);
        }
        public List<String> items() { return List.copyOf(items); } // callers can't modify ours
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cart cart = new Cart(2);
        cart.add("pen");
        cart.add("ink");
        System.out.println(cart.add("pad"));         // false: limit enforced
        cart.items().add("hack");                    // throws UnsupportedOperationException
    }
}

import java.util.List;

public class Shapes {
    static abstract class Shape {
        abstract double area();                       // subclasses must implement
        @Override public String toString() {
            return getClass().getSimpleName() + " area=" + String.format("%.1f", area());
        }
    }
    static class Circle extends Shape {
        final double r;
        Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
        @Override double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
    }
    static class Square extends Shape {
        final double side;
        Square(double side) { this.side = side; }
        @Override double area() { return side * side; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Shape> shapes = List.of(new Circle(1), new Square(2));
        for (Shape s : shapes) System.out.println(s); // Circle area=3.1, then Square area=4.0
    }
}

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Notify {
    interface Notifier {
        void send(String to, String message);
        default void sendAll(List<String> recipients, String message) {
            for (String r : recipients) send(r, message);
        }
    }
    static class ConsoleNotifier implements Notifier {
        public void send(String to, String message) { System.out.println("-> " + to + ": " + message); }
    }
    static class RecordingNotifier implements Notifier {    // handy as a test fake
        final List<String> sent = new ArrayList<>();
        public void send(String to, String message) { sent.add(to); }
    }
    static void remind(Notifier n) { n.sendAll(List.of("ana", "bo"), "standup!"); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        remind(new ConsoleNotifier());               // -> ana: standup!  -> bo: standup!
        var fake = new RecordingNotifier();
        remind(fake);
        System.out.println(fake.sent);               // [ana, bo]
    }
}

concepts.body:
An **enum** (short for enumeration) defines a type with a fixed set of named values:

```java
enum Status { PENDING, PAID, SHIPPED, CANCELLED }
```

Using `Status` instead of a `String` or `int` means a typo is caught by the compiler, your IDE can suggest the allowed values as you type, and method declarations show what's allowed.

Enums are full classes. Each constant can pass arguments to a constructor, and the enum can have fields and methods:

```java
enum Planet {
    MERCURY(3.303e23), EARTH(5.976e24);
    private final double mass;
    Planet(double mass) { this.mass = mass; }
    double mass() { return mass; }
}
```

Every enum gets useful methods for free: `values()` returns all constants in declaration order, `valueOf("PAID")` converts from a string (throwing `IllegalArgumentException` for unknown names), `name()` and `ordinal()`.

Enums are a natural fit for `switch`. A switch *expression* over an enum is **exhaustive** (has a case for every possible value) if it covers every constant, so you don't need `default` — and if someone later adds a constant, the compiler flags every switch that now misses it. That is a strong reason not to add a catch-all `default` out of habit.

Each constant exists exactly once in a running program, so comparing with `==` is correct and safe for enums.

Use `EnumMap` and `EnumSet` when keys or members are enum values; they are compact and very fast.

Don't save `ordinal()` values in a file or **database** (a program that stores data permanently): reordering the constants silently changes their meaning. Store `name()` or an explicit code field instead.

concepts.code:
public class Orders {
    enum Status {
        PENDING("waiting for payment"), PAID("paid"), SHIPPED("on its way");

        private final String label;
        Status(String label) { this.label = label; }
        String label() { return label; }
    }

    static boolean canCancel(Status s) {
        return switch (s) {            // exhaustive: no default needed
            case PENDING, PAID -> true;
            case SHIPPED -> false;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Status s : Status.values()) {
            System.out.println(s + " (" + s.label() + ") cancel=" + canCancel(s));
        }
        Status parsed = Status.valueOf("PAID");
        System.out.println(parsed == Status.PAID);   // true
    }
}

concepts.body:
A **record** (Java 16+) is a class whose purpose is to carry data. One line declares the components, and the compiler generates the rest:

```java
record Point(int x, int y) { }
```

You get: private final fields, a **canonical constructor** taking all components, accessor methods named after the components (`p.x()`, not `getX()`), and `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` based on all components. (A **hashCode** is a number summarising an object's contents, used by hash-based collections; the Maps lesson explains it.) Two records with the same values are equal, which makes them ideal map keys and test expectations.

Records are **shallowly immutable** and implicitly `final`; they can't extend another class (they implicitly extend `java.lang.Record`), but they can implement interfaces (see the Interfaces lesson).

You can add validation with a **compact constructor**, which omits the parameter list; the field assignments happen automatically at the end:

```java
record Range(int lo, int hi) {
    Range {
        if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException(lo + " > " + hi);
    }
}
```

Records can also have static fields and methods, extra instance methods, and additional constructors that delegate to the canonical one. They cannot declare additional instance fields.

"Shallowly" immutable matters: if a component is a mutable `List`, callers can still modify that list. Copy it in the compact constructor with `items = List.copyOf(items);`.

Use records for plain data: values passed between parts of a program, data received from other programs, results made of several parts, and map keys. Use a regular class when the data must change, when you need to extend another class, or when you want to hide how the data is stored.

