concepts.body:
Since Java 9 the JDK is split into **modules** (`java.base`, `java.sql`, `java.net.http`, ...), and your code can be too; this is the Java Platform Module System, **JPMS**. A module is a set of packages plus a description file, `module-info.java`, at the root of the source tree:

```java
module com.example.shop {
    requires java.net.http;
    requires transitive com.example.money;
    exports com.example.shop.api;
    opens com.example.shop.model to com.fasterxml.jackson.databind;
}
```

- `requires` — this module depends on another; missing modules fail at startup, not halfway through.
- `requires transitive` — modules that require *you* also get this dependency (use it when your public methods use that module's types).
- `exports` — packages other modules may compile against; only **exported** packages are usable from outside. Everything else, even `public` classes, is **strongly encapsulated**.
- `opens` — lets frameworks use reflection (inspecting classes while the program runs) even on private fields, without exporting the package for compilation.
- `uses` / `provides ... with` — let one module offer an implementation that another finds at runtime with `ServiceLoader`.

Code on the classpath lives in the **unnamed module**, which reads every module. That is why most applications still run unmodularised. Plain JARs placed on the module path become **automatic modules** named after the JAR (or the `Automatic-Module-Name` entry in the JAR's description file).

Even if you never write `module-info.java`, JPMS affects you: since Java 16–17, reflective access to JDK internals is denied by default. Errors like `InaccessibleObjectException ... does not "opens java.lang" to unnamed module` come from this; the fix is upgrading the library, or as a last resort `--add-opens` on the command line.

Modules also enable `jlink`, which builds a smaller custom runtime containing only the modules your app needs — handy for small Docker container images.

concepts.code:
// File: src/com.example.greet/module-info.java
module com.example.greet {
    requires java.logging;
    exports com.example.greet.api;        // public API
    // com.example.greet.internal is NOT exported: inaccessible to other modules
}

